  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 231 PM EST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis/Forecast: Early this afternoon, troughing remains \nanchored over the Northeast with ridging prevalent upstream across \nthe Plains. Heights aloft slowly in the process of rising locally, \nwith that trend continuing into early this week as ridging builds \ninto the Great Lakes. At the surface, higher pressure starting to \nfilter in from the west becomes centered overhead Sunday afternoon \nbefore sagging into the Ohio Valley early-mid week. Primary focus \nrevolves around continued potential for mainly light lake effect \nshowers/drizzle this weekend, followed by a return of well-above \nnormal temperatures for much of next week. Next chance for more \nwidespread precip looks to hold off until late week, and moreso next \nweekend.\n\nForecast Details: Low-level flow out of the northwest this afternoon \nwith H8 temps around -4C...still enough to generate a lake response \nevidenced by weak radar returns across parts of northwest lower. \nDespite higher surface pressures working in from the west, not a \nwhole lot to stop this from continuing tonight and perhaps all \nthrough way through at least the first half of Sunday. Dominant \nprecip type should largely be rain/drizzle given warmer surface \ntemperatures and a shallow moisture depth. Suppose a few flakes \ncould mix in across inland areas, but with little to no impact \nanticipated. Any of this lingering nuisance activity should wind \ndown entirely by later Sunday and especially Sunday night.\n\nNoticeable warming trend begins Monday and continues through the \nremainder of the forecast period. More sun expected for at least he \nearly and middle portions of next week as well. High temps Monday in \nthe low 50s, with many northern lower spots warming into the mid-\nupper 50s for Tuesday onward. Normal mid-November highs range from \n40-45 F area-wide. Next chance for rain returns Thursday as energy \nejects out of the Rockies ans passes by to our northwest, likely \ndragging a frontal boundary across the area locally. Longer range \ntrends favor more active weather potential returning for next \nweekend.",
